WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.930
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute Papers
with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.930 --> 00:00:05.930
Dr. Papers.

00:00:05.930 --> 00:00:11.950
Today we are going to have a look at what
is new in the physics of hair in virtual worlds.

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:17.760
You see, this is a challenging problem where
these virtual characters are given fur or

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.550
other kinds of hair, and we have to simulate
their interactions via Coulomb-friction.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:27.810
This is dry friction when two surfaces come
into contact.

00:00:27.810 --> 00:00:34.379
Which typically arises when we have lots of
hair, and thus, lots of strand-strand interactions.

00:00:34.379 --> 00:00:39.579
That happens a great deal whenever a wolf
is jumping or running around.

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:44.270
And when things start moving, these simulations
can get very, very messy.

00:00:44.270 --> 00:00:51.010
This previous paper that we talked about earlier
can resolve such conflicts, and create beautiful,

00:00:51.010 --> 00:00:52.989
realistic hair simulations.

00:00:52.989 --> 00:00:57.890
However, this class of algorithms typically
takes forever to compute.

00:00:57.890 --> 00:01:01.600
They are typically not even close to real
time.

00:01:01.600 --> 00:01:07.540
However, NVIDIA has also done some research
on it, these algorithms typically run on your

00:01:07.540 --> 00:01:14.630
graphics card, and are fast enough to be deployed
in real video games and other virtual worlds.

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:20.270
But wait, these can not resolve scenes that
are as detailed as the ones in the previous

00:01:20.270 --> 00:01:21.270
paper.

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:27.210
With this, we have to resort to much simpler
models and fewer hair strands, many of which

00:01:27.210 --> 00:01:29.390
typically move together.

00:01:29.390 --> 00:01:34.259
To the best of my knowledge, they also haven’t
seen a lot of improvements over the last few

00:01:34.259 --> 00:01:35.259
years.

00:01:35.259 --> 00:01:37.090
So, are we done?

00:01:37.090 --> 00:01:41.790
Is there no hope for quick and detailed hair
simulations?

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:46.240
Well, sometimes, all it takes is just one
person.

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:47.240
One scientist.

00:01:47.240 --> 00:01:54.480
Yes, you are seeing correctly, this is a one-author
SIGGRAPH paper from NVIDIA on this exact topic,

00:01:54.480 --> 00:01:57.670
so I am itching to look at it together.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:01:59.040
And…whoa.

00:01:59.040 --> 00:02:02.899
Now that is a next-generation hair simulation
right there!

00:02:02.899 --> 00:02:07.789
It looks like almost every single hair strand
is simulated independently.

00:02:07.789 --> 00:02:09.860
My goodness!

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:15.459
And as I am a light transport researcher by
trade, I have to note that I love how the

00:02:15.459 --> 00:02:18.220
specular highlights are computed here.

00:02:18.220 --> 00:02:23.080
And what you see here is the favorite pastime
of the computer graphics researcher.

00:02:23.080 --> 00:02:24.180
And here.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:30.879
Yes, this time I hope it will not get as painful
as with some of the previous papers.

00:02:30.879 --> 00:02:36.770
But it looks like this character is getting
away by just getting its hair cut.

00:02:36.770 --> 00:02:38.750
Very fun experiment indeed.

00:02:38.750 --> 00:02:44.379
And now comes the bombshell: These models
have thousands of hair strands that are made

00:02:44.379 --> 00:02:52.489
up of 120,000 points, that is incredible,
so now I feel that it’s appropriate to pop

00:02:52.489 --> 00:02:53.530
the question.

00:02:53.530 --> 00:02:57.360
So, it does all this how quickly exactly?

00:02:57.360 --> 00:03:04.970
Well, hold on to your papers Fellow Scholars,
because it does all this in 17 milliseconds.

00:03:04.970 --> 00:03:08.650
Yes, all this is happening in real time.

00:03:08.650 --> 00:03:12.840
And these are not some run of the mill simulations
that we saw earlier.

00:03:12.840 --> 00:03:14.060
No sir!

00:03:14.060 --> 00:03:15.989
These are super detailed.

00:03:15.989 --> 00:03:22.019
And if we pop the hood and look at the internal
workings of the algorithm, we see that most

00:03:22.019 --> 00:03:28.700
of the time is spent with detecting and resolving
these collisions, and oh my goodness.

00:03:28.700 --> 00:03:31.060
Are you seeing what I am seeing?

00:03:31.060 --> 00:03:37.370
Oh yes, depending on the current state of
the hair, there is substantial variation between

00:03:37.370 --> 00:03:40.370
the execution times of the algorithm.

00:03:40.370 --> 00:03:42.110
That is big trouble!

00:03:42.110 --> 00:03:43.110
Why?

00:03:43.110 --> 00:03:49.209
Well, because if we are in any kind of virtual
world or we are playing a video game, we have

00:03:49.209 --> 00:03:55.390
to make sure that every individual picture
is computed quickly and on time.

00:03:55.390 --> 00:04:01.689
If one moment we have 10 frames per second,
and the next moment we have 60, what we get

00:04:01.689 --> 00:04:03.459
is a jarring experience.

00:04:03.459 --> 00:04:05.510
So, are we done?

00:04:05.510 --> 00:04:07.260
Is this not going to make it?

00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:14.680
Do not despair for a second, because most
of the time it runs so quickly, it only takes

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:20.350
a couple milliseconds, and sometimes not even
a millisecond.

00:04:20.350 --> 00:04:26.380
So I think this one is going to do just fine
and hopefully will be implemented in the video

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:28.430
games of the future.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:30.010
What a time to be alive!

00:04:30.010 --> 00:04:34.710
But just one more quick question - is this
actually accurate?

00:04:34.710 --> 00:04:40.610
Well, let’s have a closer look at these
two lines, and there we have our answer.

00:04:40.610 --> 00:04:46.380
Now, I hear you saying, Dr. Papers, why are
you saying that there are two lines here,

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.509
I see only one!

00:04:48.509 --> 00:04:50.500
And that is exactly the point.

00:04:50.500 --> 00:04:56.830
The simulated results are so close to reality
that the measurements essentially overlap.

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:59.370
And all that in real time.

00:04:59.370 --> 00:05:01.350
That is incredible.

00:05:01.350 --> 00:05:06.789
And now you can see that the papers that we
discuss here are real, they get out of there

00:05:06.789 --> 00:05:11.980
in the real world to be used in real products
by millions of people.

00:05:11.980 --> 00:05:17.460
And sometimes, all this comes from the ingenuity
of one Fellow Scholar.

00:05:17.460 --> 00:05:21.060
One scientist is all it takes to change everything.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.680
Huge congratulations!

00:05:22.680 --> 00:06:09.330
Thanks for watching and for your generous
support, and I'll see you 

00:06:09.330 --> 00:06:18.229
next time!

